But also, x itself added once — but if x appears also as x//d for some d? x//d = x implies d=1, not in range. Fine. Also note: for x=2: isqrt=1, loop range(2,2) empty, add divisor 2. Good. x=4: isqrt=2, d=2: 4%2=0, add 2, q=2==d skip. add 4. Divisors {2,4} ✓.

Now double-check M ≥ ... f for each k is best which is ≥ nums[j_first] ≥ 2. So M ≥ 2 > -mn, no need for the -mn max. But safe to leave out since we proved it.

Now also handle the all-ones case: return (-mn * 2) % MOD = (-2) % MOD. But wait, is k=2 the smallest k when all ones? k>1, smallest is 2, and f(2) = -1 = M (all k give -1). Yes.

